` would be a dangling reference."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Panics"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Implementations should generally avoid [`panic!`]ing, because `drop()` may itself be called"},{"DocComment":" during unwinding due to a panic, and if the `drop()` panics in that situation (a “double"},{"DocComment":" panic”), this will likely abort the program. It is possible to check [`panicking()`] first,"},{"DocComment":" which may be desirable for a `Drop` implementation that is reporting a bug of the kind"},{"DocComment":" “you didn't finish using this before it was dropped”; but most types should simply clean up"},{"DocComment":" their owned allocations or other resources and return normally from `drop()`, regardless of"},{"DocComment":" what state they are in."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that even if this panics, the value is considered to be dropped;"},{"DocComment":" you must not cause `drop` to be called again. This is normally automatically"},{"DocComment":" handled by the compiler, but when using unsafe code, can sometimes occur"},{"DocComment":" unintentionally, particularly when using [`ptr::drop_in_place`]."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [E0040]: ../../error_codes/E0040.html"},{"DocComment":" [`panic!`]: crate::panic!"},{"DocComment":" [`panicking()`]: ../../std/thread/fn.panicking.html"},{"DocComment":" [`mem::drop`]: drop"},{"DocComment":" [`ptr::drop_in_place`]: crate::ptr::drop_in_place"}],"inline":null,"rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":null},"generics":{"regions":[{"index":0,"name":null,"mutability":"Unknown"}],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"Self"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[{"clause_id":0,"span":{"data":{"file_id":0,"beg":{"line":1,"col":0},"end":{"line":1,"col":0}},"generated_from_span":null},"origin":"TraitSelf","trait_":{"regions":[],"skip_binder":{"id":50,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":20677}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}}}}],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"signature":{"is_unsafe":false,"abi":"Rust","is_variadic":false,"inputs":[{"Deduplicated":20906}],"output":{"Deduplicated":1938}},"src":{"TraitDecl":{"trait_ref":{"id":50,"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"Deduplicated":20677}],"const_generics":[],"trait_refs":[]}},"item_id":{"Method":0}}},"is_global_initializer":null,"body":"Opaque"},null,{"def_id":311,"item_meta":{"name":[{"Ident":["core",0]},{"Ident":["mem",0]},{"Ident":["needs_drop",0]}],"span":{"data":{"file_id":46,"beg":{"line":661,"col":0},"end":{"line":661,"col":44}},"generated_from_span":null},"source_text":null,"attr_info":{"attributes":[{"DocComment":" Returns `true` if dropping values of type `T` matters."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" This is purely an optimization hint, and may be implemented conservatively:"},{"DocComment":" it may return `true` for types that don't actually need to be dropped."},{"DocComment":" As such always returning `true` would be a valid implementation of"},{"DocComment":" this function. However if this function actually returns `false`, then you"},{"DocComment":" can be certain dropping `T` has no side effect."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Low level implementations of things like collections, which need to manually"},{"DocComment":" drop their data, should use this function to avoid unnecessarily"},{"DocComment":" trying to drop all their contents when they are destroyed. This might not"},{"DocComment":" make a difference in release builds (where a loop that has no side-effects"},{"DocComment":" is easily detected and eliminated), but is often a big win for debug builds."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Note that [`drop_in_place`] already performs this check, so if your workload"},{"DocComment":" can be reduced to some small number of [`drop_in_place`] calls, using this is"},{"DocComment":" unnecessary. In particular note that you can [`drop_in_place`] a slice, and that"},{"DocComment":" will do a single needs_drop check for all the values."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Types like Vec therefore just `drop_in_place(&mut self[..])` without using"},{"DocComment":" `needs_drop` explicitly. Types like [`HashMap`], on the other hand, have to drop"},{"DocComment":" values one at a time and should use this API."},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" [`drop_in_place`]: crate::ptr::drop_in_place"},{"DocComment":" [`HashMap`]: ../../std/collections/struct.HashMap.html"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" # Examples"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" Here's an example of how a collection might make use of `needs_drop`:"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" ```"},{"DocComment":" use std::{mem, ptr};"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" pub struct MyCollection<T> {"},{"DocComment":" #   data: [T; 1],"},{"DocComment":"     /* ... */"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" # impl<T> MyCollection<T> {"},{"DocComment":" #   fn iter_mut(&mut self) -> &mut [T] { &mut self.data }"},{"DocComment":" #   fn free_buffer(&mut self) {}"},{"DocComment":" # }"},{"DocComment":""},{"DocComment":" impl<T> Drop for MyCollection<T> {"},{"DocComment":"     fn drop(&mut self) {"},{"DocComment":"         unsafe {"},{"DocComment":"             // drop the data"},{"DocComment":"             if mem::needs_drop::<T>() {"},{"DocComment":"                 for x in self.iter_mut() {"},{"DocComment":"                     ptr::drop_in_place(x);"},{"DocComment":"                 }"},{"DocComment":"             }"},{"DocComment":"             self.free_buffer();"},{"DocComment":"         }"},{"DocComment":"     }"},{"DocComment":" }"},{"DocComment":" ```"}],"inline":"Hint","rename":null,"public":true},"is_local":false,"opacity":"Foreign","lang_item":"needs_drop"},"generics":{"regions":[],"types":[{"index":0,"name":"T"}],"const_generics":[],"trait_clauses":[],"regions_outlive":[],"types_outlive":[],"trait_type_constraints":[]},"signature":{"is_unsafe":false,"abi":"Rust","is_variadic":false,"inputs":[],"output":{"D